/* The two warm hero blobs were `filter: blur(100px)` / `blur(80px)` over a solid
   circle until 2026-07-21. A large-radius blur is rastered on the main thread
   before first paint and these sit above the fold, so they were gating homepage
   LCP. They are now pre-baked radial-gradients (see the inline styles on the
   .hero__deco divs): visually equivalent, effectively free to raster.

   The geometry is DERIVED, not eyeballed. A CSS blur radius is the Gaussian
   sigma, so colour spreads ~3σ past the element box — the visible blob was far
   bigger than its 300px/200px div. Each replacement box is therefore grown to
   (original + 3σ per side) and re-anchored on the SAME centre via calc(), with
   gradient stops approximating the blurred disc: a near-solid core, falling off
   to transparent inside the box. Do NOT "simplify" these back to a small box
   with a blur filter — that is the regression this fixed.
   See docs/plans/2026-07-21-homepage-lcp-critical-css.md § Step 1.

   `.ambient-orb` below still uses blur(80px) deliberately: it is below the fold,
   where paint cost is not on the LCP path. */
/* Metric-matched fallback for Outfit (added 2026-07-22). THIS FIXES REAL CLS —
   it is not a cosmetic nicety, and removing it regresses the homepage.

   Measured cause: with no fallback face, `Outfit` in the stacks resolves to
   nothing and text renders in the platform default (DejaVu Sans on Linux),
   which is ~19% WIDER than Outfit. The hero is centre-aligned, so when Outfit
   finished loading every line narrowed and slid horizontally — .btn-primary
   alone scored 0.0979. Note the shift is horizontal; nothing moves vertically.

   Two levers, both needed:
     1. Pin the fallback to Arial-metric faces instead of the platform default.
        Arimo / Liberation Sans / Nimbus Sans / Helvetica / Arial all share
        Arial's advance widths, so one tuning covers Linux, Windows and macOS.
        This alone cuts the width delta from ~19% to ~4.4%.
     2. size-adjust closes the rest. Measured Outfit-vs-Arial-metric width
        ratios by canvas measureText at the weights the hero actually uses:
        800 -> 0.9663, 700 -> 0.9535, 600 -> 0.9194, 400 -> 0.9867. A single
        scalar cannot match all four; 95.5% is weighted toward the large-area
        elements (headline 800, button 700) that dominate the CLS score.

   Verified outcome: homepage CLS 0.1868 -> 0.0075 (median of 5, range
   0.0056-0.0075), LCP unchanged at 2709ms, perf score 0.86 -> 0.94. The
   correction is approximate and slightly overshoots — the fallback now renders
   a little NARROWER than Outfit (button 234px vs 245px) where it was 40px wider
   before. Matching every weight exactly would need one face per weight range;
   at 13x under the 0.1 budget that is not worth the complexity.

   ascent/descent overrides are the Outfit values (1.0em / 0.26em) divided by
   size-adjust, so the fallback's line box matches Outfit's exactly. Our hero
   elements all set an explicit numeric line-height so this is belt-and-braces,
   but it keeps the face correct for any Outfit text that does not.

   Recompute these if Outfit is ever bumped. Do NOT rename the family to bare
   'Outfit': that name is live at discord.astro:35 and plausible-exclude.astro,
   which would silently start rendering in this fallback instead of the brand
   face. Full derivation + the rejected alternatives (font preload costs +377ms
   LCP) in docs/plans/2026-07-22-lighthouse-gate-honesty-and-homepage-cls.md */
@font-face {
  font-family: 'Outfit Fallback';
  src:
    local('Arimo'), local('Liberation Sans'), local('Nimbus Sans'), local('Helvetica Neue'),
    local('Helvetica'), local('Arial');
  size-adjust: 95.5%;
  ascent-override: 104.7%;
  descent-override: 27.2%;
  line-gap-override: 0%;
}

/* Containing block for every decorative absolute in the hero (added 2026-07-22).
   THIS FIXES A REAL CLS DEFECT — do not remove it as redundant markup.

   `.hero` is `min-height: 100vh` but on a phone its CONTENT is taller than the
   viewport, so the section's height is content-driven. Ten decorations were
   anchored on percentages of that height (`top: 60%`, `bottom: 35%`,
   `top: calc(40% - 240px)`, …). When Outfit/Inter finished loading, the headline
   reflowed, the section grew, every percentage re-resolved, and a 680px blob
   jumped — measured CLS 0.1868, reproducible on every run, ~2x the 0.1 budget.

   Anchoring them to this 100vh layer instead makes the percentages resolve
   against something font-loading cannot change. No child's inline values were
   touched. `100vh` NOT `100dvh`: dvh shrinks as mobile browser chrome hides on
   scroll, which would reintroduce the shift. `.hero` keeps `overflow: hidden`,
   so clipping is unchanged and this layer deliberately sets no overflow. */
